WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.750
The requested title uses STL, and many developers still use that term in everyday conversation.

2
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:13.416
Modern teams often talk more broadly about the C++ standard library, including containers, strings, views, spans, algorithms, smart pointers, and

3
00:00:13.416 --> 00:00:14.182
filesystem utilities.

4
00:00:14.182 --> 00:00:19.165
This course uses STL as shorthand while focusing on practical standard-library use.

5
00:00:19.165 --> 00:00:25.682
The standard library is a major safety improvement over manual arrays, manual memory management, and handwritten algorithms.

6
00:00:25.682 --> 00:00:31.431
Containers manage storage, strings preserve length, algorithms express intent, and wrappers can make ownership clearer.

7
00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:34.881
These abstractions give reviewers more structure to reason about.

8
00:00:34.881 --> 00:00:38.331
But the library does not automatically make code safe.

9
00:00:38.331 --> 00:00:41.781
A vector can still be indexed out of range.

10
00:00:41.781 --> 00:00:45.231
A string_view can still outlive the string it observes.

11
00:00:45.231 --> 00:00:48.680
An iterator can still become invalid after a mutation.

12
00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.280
A comparator can still violate the rules an ordered container depends on.

13
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.730
Secure STL usage means respecting each abstraction's contract.

14
00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:02.863
The important questions are lifetime, bounds, ownership, invalidation, resource growth, exception behavior, and thread-safety assumptions.

15
00:01:02.863 --> 00:01:07.462
Those questions do not disappear just because the code uses standard types.

16
00:01:07.462 --> 00:01:12.445
A good review does not ask only whether a standard container is present.

17
00:01:12.445 --> 00:01:21.645
It asks whether the chosen type makes the intended access pattern clear, whether sizes and indexes are validated, whether views are short-lived, and

18
00:01:21.645 --> 00:01:23.561
whether mutations invalidate cached access.

19
00:01:23.561 --> 00:01:31.228
The goal is defensive engineering: use standard-library tools to reduce manual mistakes, then combine them with explicit contracts, tests,

20
00:01:31.228 --> 00:01:33.144
sanitizers, static analysis, and review.

21
00:01:33.144 --> 00:01:38.894
A useful mindset is to treat every standard-library type as an abstraction with rules.

22
00:01:38.894 --> 00:01:46.560
The more important the data or boundary, the more clearly those rules should appear in naming, API shape, and tests.
